---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: System Design Guide
description: A free, organised study guide for system design interviews. Fundamentals with diagrams and trade-offs, ten worked interview questions, an interactive Design Lab, and an Anki deck.
section: home
---
<div class="home">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Free study guide · system design interviews</p>
    <h1>Learn to design large-scale systems from scratch.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Every building block explained plainly: what it is, when to use it and what it costs, with a diagram for each. Ten classic interview questions worked through step by step. And a Design Lab where you build, size and stress-test your own designs.</p>
    <div class="hero__cta">
      <a class="btn btn--berry" href="/lab/">Open the Design Lab</a>
      <a class="btn" href="{{ collections.topics[0].url }}">Start with the fundamentals</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="{{ collections.practice[0].url }}">Practice questions</a>
    </div>
    <p class="press" aria-hidden="true">▶ PRESS START<span class="blink">_</span></p>
    <div class="stats">
      <span><b>{{ collections.topics.length }}</b> fundamentals</span>
      <span><b>{{ collections.practice.length }}</b> worked questions</span>
      <span><b>{{ lab.scenarios.length }}</b> lab scenarios</span>
      <span><b>{{ flashcards.length }}</b> flashcards</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="lab">
    <div class="feature__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Featured · interactive</p>
      <h2 id="lab">The Design Lab</h2>
      <p class="feature__lede">Reading designs is one thing; making one is what the interview tests. Pick a real brief, build your answer on a canvas, and find out how it holds up.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="feature__list">
      <li style="--k: var(--k-lb)">{{ lab.icons.lb | safe }}<span><b>Build it.</b> Drag load balancers, servers, databases, caches and queues onto a canvas, wire them up, and pick the real tech: PostgreSQL or Cassandra, Redis or Memcached, Kafka or SQS.</span></li>
      <li style="--k: var(--k-db)">{{ lab.icons.sql | safe }}<span><b>Get rated.</b> A rank from S to D, what works, what's missing and why, and the anti-patterns that cost you. Then compare with the best design.</span></li>
      <li style="--k: var(--k-service)">{{ lab.icons.app | safe }}<span><b>Size it.</b> From users and the work per request, see how many servers, load balancers, shards, replicas and cache nodes you need.</span></li>
      <li style="--k: var(--k-cache)">{{ lab.icons.cache | safe }}<span><b>Guess first.</b> Estimate before the reveal. Way too many and it blasts (wasted money); way too few and it melts down.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="hero__cta">
      <a class="btn btn--berry" href="/lab/">Open the Design Lab</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/lab/?s=url-shortener">Try the URL shortener</a>
    </div>
    {%- set demo = demos.lab %}
    {% include "partials/demo.njk" %}
  </section>

  <h2 id="study-plan">A study plan</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Roughly three to four weeks at an hour a day. Tick pages off with “Mark as done” at the bottom of each one; your progress is saved in this browser.</p>
  <ol class="plan">
    <li><b>Learn the method.</b> Read <a href="/topics/how-to-approach/">how to approach the interview</a> and <a href="/topics/estimation/">back-of-the-envelope estimation</a>. Every answer in this guide follows that framework.</li>
    <li><b>Foundations.</b> Networking, scaling and load balancing: how a request reaches your servers, and how you add more of them.</li>
    <li><b>Data.</b> Databases, indexing, replication, sharding, consistent hashing and the CAP theorem. This is where most interview trade-offs live.</li>
    <li><b>Speed and decoupling.</b> Caching, CDNs, message queues, APIs and rate limiting.</li>
    <li><b>The rest of the toolkit.</b> Object storage, unique IDs and reliability patterns.</li>
    <li><b>Practise out loud.</b> For each question, set a 45-minute timer and sketch your own answer <em>before</em> reading the walkthrough. Then compare.</li>
    <li><b>Review daily.</b> Ten minutes of <a href="/flashcards/">flashcards</a> (or the Anki deck) keeps the terms and numbers fresh.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="fundamentals">Fundamentals</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Each page covers what the concept is, how it works, the pros and cons, and what interviewers listen for.</p>
  <div class="grid">
    {%- for t in collections.topics %}
    <a class="tile" href="{{ t.url }}" data-tile="{{ t.url }}">
      <span class="tile__num">{{ t.data.order | pad2 }}</span>
      <h3>{{ t.data.navTitle or t.data.title }}</h3>
      <p>{{ t.data.summary }}</p>
    </a>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice questions</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Full walkthroughs: requirements, estimates, API, data model, high-level design, deep dives and failure modes, with the reasoning spoken out loud.</p>
  <div class="grid">
    {%- for t in collections.practice %}
    <a class="tile" href="{{ t.url }}" data-tile="{{ t.url }}">
      <span class="tile__num">Q{{ t.data.order }} · {{ t.data.difficulty }}</span>
      <h3>{{ t.data.navTitle or t.data.title }}</h3>
      <p>{{ t.data.summary }}</p>
    </a>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>

  <h2 id="review">Review with flashcards</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Key terms and concepts from every page. Play them on the pocket console, or download the Anki deck for spaced repetition.</p>
  <div class="hero__cta">
    <a class="btn btn--berry" href="/flashcards/">Play the flashcards</a>
    <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/assets/anki/system-design-guide.apkg" download>Download the Anki deck</a>
  </div>
</div>
